{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case Five.",
"start": 0.79,
"end": 2.25,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Marrow Mix-up.",
"start": 3.13,
"end": 4.911,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "Hedley Truscott had grown the largest marrow anyone in Tidewhistle Cove had ever seen. It was long and green and striped, and it lay in his allotment shed on a bed of straw, waiting for the vegetable show on Saturday.",
"start": 4.911,
"end": 19.47,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "On Thursday morning, the straw was empty.",
"start": 20.63,
"end": 22.85,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Four people had been at the allotments the evening before: Jago Penhallow, Tamsin Trevelyan, Morwenna Day, and Hedley himself, who had locked up last. The shed lock was old, and Hedley admitted that anybody with a firm tug could have opened it. Hedley wanted to be fair, so he counted himself as a suspect too.",
"start": 23.99,
"end": 43.57,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes Bell sat them all down on the bench outside the shed with a flask of tea.",
"start": 44.65,
"end": 49.07,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"I know this village,\" she said. \"Whoever took that marrow will tell me a fib. Everybody else will tell me the plain truth. So let's hear from each of you, one at a time.\"",
"start": 50.19,
"end": 60.23,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Jago went first. \"It wasn't Hedley. He'd never pinch his own marrow.\"",
"start": 61.27,
"end": 65.19,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Tamsin went next. \"It was either Jago or Morwenna.\"",
"start": 66.51,
"end": 69.41,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Hedley said, \"Tamsin is telling the truth.\"",
"start": 70.47,
"end": 72.93,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Morwenna said, \"It wasn't me. And it wasn't Tamsin, either.\"",
"start": 74.17,
"end": 77.47,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes poured another cup and thought it through.",
"start": 78.73,
"end": 80.69,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Here are the statements once more. Jago: it wasn't Hedley. Tamsin: it was Jago or Morwenna. Hedley: Tamsin is telling the truth. Morwenna: it wasn't me, and it wasn't Tamsin. Only the thief is lying. Everyone else tells the truth.",
"start": 81.79,
"end": 98.27,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? Who took the prize marrow?",
"start": 99.57,
"end": 101.61,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 103.11,
"end": 104.07,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 105.01,
"end": 109.97,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 114.41,
"end": 115.45,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Morwenna Day took the marrow.",
"start": 115.45,
"end": 118.31,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Try each person as the thief. If Jago were the thief, he would be lying, so it would have been Hedley. But then Jago would not be the thief after all. That doesn't work.",
"start": 119.57,
"end": 129.23,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "If Tamsin were the thief, her statement would be a lie, which fits. But then Hedley, who is innocent, said Tamsin was telling the truth. That would make Hedley a liar too, and only the thief lies. That doesn't work.",
"start": 130.29,
"end": 143.37,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "If Hedley were the thief, then Tamsin's statement, Jago or Morwenna, would be false, and Tamsin would be an innocent liar. That doesn't work either.",
"start": 144.37,
"end": 153.67,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "If Morwenna is the thief, everything fits. Jago is right that it wasn't Hedley. Tamsin is right that it was Jago or Morwenna. Hedley is right that Tamsin is telling the truth. And Morwenna's own statement, it wasn't me, is the one lie.",
"start": 154.73,
"end": 170.41,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Morwenna confessed with a sigh. She had needed something spectacular for the centre of the harvest flower display, and the marrow was simply too beautiful to resist. It went back to Hedley's straw, and on Saturday it won first prize.",
"start": 171.45,
"end": 185.59,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
